Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage
• legislators_terms.id_bioguide : unique person identifier – lets us group all terms that belong to one legislator.  
• legislators_terms.term_number : ordinal of a person’s career term – used to pick the “first” term (term_number = 0).  
• legislators_terms.term_start : start date of the term – filter cohort whose very first term_start falls between 1917‑01‑01 and 1999‑12‑31 (inclusive).  
• legislators_terms.term_end : end date of a term – test whether a legislator is still in office on each 31‑Dec of the 20‑year window following the first term_start.  
• legislators_terms.term_type : can separate House (“rep”) versus Senate (“sen”) if we later want to analyse sub‑groups (not strictly needed for the overall retention calculation).  

Typical conditions we will need  
• term_number = 0                                    -- selects each legislator’s first term row  
• term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'   -- defines the cohort  
• date('YYYY-MM-DD') BETWEEN term_start AND term_end -- checks whether a legislator is in office on a given 31‑Dec reference date  

Exploratory SQL queries  
(The following 10 queries, each different, increase in complexity and help familiarise ourselves with the data before building the final retention‑rate statement. All queries use DISTINCT where possible and are capped with LIMIT 20.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the range of term_start values available
SELECT DISTINCT term_start
FROM legislators_terms
ORDER BY term_start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Verify the maximum and minimum term_start dates
SELECT
       MIN(term_start) AS earliest_start,
       MAX(term_start) AS latest_start
FROM legislators_terms;
```

```sql
-- 3. List all first‑term rows that belong to our cohort window
SELECT DISTINCT id_bioguide, term_start, term_end
FROM legislators_terms
WHERE term_number = 0
  AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Count how many legislators are in that cohort
SELECT COUNT(*) AS cohort_size
FROM legislators_terms
WHERE term_number = 0
  AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31';
```

```sql
-- 5. For one sample legislator, look at every term row to understand overlaps
SELECT *
FROM legislators_terms
WHERE id_bioguide = (
        SELECT id_bioguide
        FROM legislators_terms
        WHERE term_number = 0
          AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
        LIMIT 1
      )
ORDER BY term_start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Determine, for each legislator in the cohort, the date of their last recorded term_end
SELECT id_bioguide,
       MAX(term_end) AS final_end
FROM legislators_terms
WHERE id_bioguide IN (
        SELECT id_bioguide
        FROM legislators_terms
        WHERE term_number = 0
          AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
      )
GROUP BY id_bioguide
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Example: check whether cohort members are still serving 1 year (Dec 31) after first term_start
SELECT lt.id_bioguide,
       lt.term_start AS first_start,
       date(lt.term_start, '+1 year', 'start of year', '+364 days') AS ref_date,
       CASE
           WHEN date(lt.term_start, '+1 year', 'start of year', '+364 days')
                BETWEEN lt.term_start AND (SELECT MAX(term_end)
                                           FROM legislators_terms AS lt2
                                           WHERE lt2.id_bioguide = lt.id_bioguide)
           THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS retained
FROM legislators_terms AS lt
WHERE lt.term_number = 0
  AND lt.term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Aggregate: proportion retained 1 year later
SELECT
       ROUND(AVG(
             CASE
               WHEN date(term_start, '+1 year', 'start of year', '+364 days')
                    BETWEEN term_start
                    AND (SELECT MAX(term_end)
                         FROM legislators_terms AS t2
                         WHERE t2.id_bioguide = t1.id_bioguide)
               THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
       ), 4) AS retention_after_1yr
FROM legislators_terms AS t1
WHERE term_number = 0
  AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31';
```

```sql
-- 9. Build a small calendar table (1–20 years) on‑the‑fly and test one legislator
WITH years(n) AS (
      SELECT 0 UNION ALL SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL
      SELECT 3 UNION ALL SELECT 4 UNION ALL SELECT 5 UNION ALL
      SELECT 6 UNION ALL SELECT 7 UNION ALL SELECT 8 UNION ALL
      SELECT 9 UNION ALL SELECT 10 UNION ALL SELECT 11 UNION ALL
      SELECT 12 UNION ALL SELECT 13 UNION ALL SELECT 14 UNION ALL
      SELECT 15 UNION ALL SELECT 16 UNION ALL SELECT 17 UNION ALL
      SELECT 18 UNION ALL SELECT 19 UNION ALL SELECT 20
)
SELECT y.n AS yrs_after_start,
       date(lt.term_start, '+'||y.n||' years', 'start of year', '+364 days') AS ref_date
FROM legislators_terms AS lt
JOIN years AS y
WHERE lt.id_bioguide = (
        SELECT id_bioguide
        FROM legislators_terms
        WHERE term_number = 0
          AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
        LIMIT 1
      )
  AND lt.term_number = 0
ORDER BY y.n
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Framework for full retention calculation (shows the skeleton; results limited for inspection)
WITH cohort AS (
     SELECT id_bioguide,
            term_start
     FROM legislators_terms
     WHERE term_number = 0
       AND term_start BETWEEN '1917-01-01' AND '1999-12-31'
),
years(n) AS (
     SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL SELECT 3 UNION ALL
     SELECT 4 UNION ALL SELECT 5 UNION ALL SELECT 6 UNION ALL
     SELECT 7 UNION ALL SELECT 8 UNION ALL SELECT 9 UNION ALL
     SELECT 10 UNION ALL SELECT 11 UNION ALL SELECT 12 UNION ALL
     SELECT 13 UNION ALL SELECT 14 UNION ALL SELECT 15 UNION ALL
     SELECT 16 UNION ALL SELECT 17 UNION ALL SELECT 18 UNION ALL
     SELECT 19 UNION ALL SELECT 20
)
SELECT y.n AS year_after_start,
       ROUND(AVG( CASE
           WHEN date(c.term_start, '+'||y.n||' years', 'start of year', '+364 days')
                BETWEEN c.term_start
                AND (SELECT MAX(term_end)
                     FROM legislators_terms AS t
                     WHERE t.id_bioguide = c.id_bioguide)
           THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
       ), 4) AS retention_rate
FROM cohort AS c
CROSS JOIN years AS y
GROUP BY y.n
ORDER BY y.n
LIMIT 20;
```

These exploratory statements progressively inspect values, validate the cohort definition, and prototype the logic that will ultimately yield the requested 20‑year retention‑rate sequence.
